\f0\fs22
IntelliFire Fireplace (Wi-Fi Module) \endash  Control4 Driver\par
\par
Unofficial / community driver. Not affiliated with or supported by Hearth & Home Technologies or Control4.\par
\par
v102: fixed a reconnect bug where the driver would stop sending requests after the first connection cycle.\par
v103: API User ID / API Key are now trimmed of leading/trailing whitespace on load, so a stray space from copy/paste can't silently break command signing.\par
v104: switched from a light_v2 (dimmer) proxy to a thermostatV2 proxy. light_v2 devices get pulled into a room's Lighting group, so \ldblquote All Lights Off\rdblquote  scenes and lighting keypads would also control the fireplace; thermostatV2 avoids that, matching how commercial Control4 fireplace drivers (e.g. Elite Fire Proflame2) do it. HVAC Mode Off/Heat now turns the fireplace off/on, and the Heat Setpoint (0-100) controls flame height in place of the old dimmer level. Pilot on/off is unchanged, still a separate Composer command/action. This hardware has no temperature sensor, so the current-temperature readout will show blank - expected.\par
v105: fixed a bug found in live testing where the driver could get permanently stuck (Connection Status pinned OFFLINE, no further polls or commands) if the connection dropped before a request's response fully arrived. The interrupted request is now automatically requeued and retried instead of leaving the driver stuck until manually restarted.\par
v106: fixed the real bug behind commands not working at all - the local /post command signing scheme was wrong (confirmed live: the real fireplace returned 403 Forbidden on every command). Replaced with the actual algorithm from intellifire4py's reference implementation: a nested double-SHA-256 with a \ldblquote post:\rdblquote  prefix on the hashed payload, not the single SHA-256 this driver used before. Also fixed the flame-height command name (\ldblquote flame_height\rdblquote , not \ldblquote height\rdblquote  - that name is only used in /poll responses, not for setting it).\par
v107: removed an invented ignition sequence (light pilot, wait 5s, then send power) that was never vendor-confirmed. Confirmed against intellifire4py's real control.py that turning the fireplace on is just a direct power=1 command - the firmware handles its own ignition sequence internally. Pilot on/off is unaffected, still available as its own Composer command/action.\par
v108: fixed the ~20s lag between changing Mode/flame height in Navigator and seeing it take effect. Two changes: the thermostat card now updates optimistically the instant a command is issued (rather than waiting on a full network round trip, each of which needs its own reconnect on this hardware), and multi-step actions like turning off (which sends both power=0 and pilot=0) now trigger a single debounced status refresh instead of one per sub-command.\par
v109: changed the Heat Setpoint dial from a 0-100 percentage to 1-5, matching the fireplace's 5 real flame-height levels (the same numbering used on IntelliFire's own remote). Dragging the dial to any of the 5 steps now turns the flame on directly to that height, the same way dragging a dimmer up turns a light on; use Mode Off/Heat to turn the fireplace fully off. The setpoint keeps showing the last flame-height level even while Mode is Off.\par
v110: fixed Navigator not showing Heat as on even though the fireplace audibly started igniting. The single reconcile poll (1.5s after a command) often caught the real hardware still mid-ignition/purge - which takes noticeably longer than 1.5s - and that stale "still off" snapshot was being trusted as ground truth, silently reverting the optimistic Heat display back to Off. Now a poll that doesn\rquote t yet match the just-issued command is treated as "not caught up yet" rather than "the command failed": the driver quietly rechecks every 2 seconds (up to about 27 seconds total) until a poll actually confirms the change, instead of reverting the display after one early look.\par
v111: fixed the Heat Setpoint dial not working in the Control4 App - dragging it didn\rquote t actually change the flame height. Root cause: the real thermostatV2 setpoint command sends the value as CELSIUS/FAHRENHEIT params, not a "SETPOINT" param - confirmed against a real published thermostatV2 driver\rquote s source code. This driver was reading a "SETPOINT" param that Navigator/the App never actually sends, so every drag silently fell back to the lowest flame height no matter where the dial was set. Also added a couple of setpoint capability fields that were missing compared to the reference driver.\par
v112: fixed the thermostat card appearing then immediately disappearing in the Control4 App on iPad right after setting Heat. Root cause: this fireplace\rquote s Wi-Fi module closes its TCP connection after every single request (by design), so the driver reported CONNECTION=false to the proxy after essentially every command and every poll - even though it reconnects again a fraction of a second later. The Control4 App reacts to CONNECTION=false by hiding the device outright rather than just greying it out, which is exactly what going from Off to Heat (two quick commands, two quick disconnects) would trigger. A "really offline" notification is now delayed by a few seconds and only sent if the connection is still down by then, so momentary per-request drops never reach the app at all - only genuine outages do.\par
v113: fixed the flame-height dial appearing to "default to 1" in the app. Root cause: right when the driver starts up, it announced the flame height as a hardcoded placeholder (0, shown as "1" on the 1-5 dial) immediately - before it had ever actually asked the real fireplace what height it was really at. That placeholder got sent to Navigator/the App as if it were real data, and only got silently corrected a moment later once the first real poll came back. Now the driver stays quiet about mode and flame height until the first real poll actually confirms them (the same way it already handled the connection status), so the app never sees a guessed value - only the fireplace\rquote s real, polled height.\par
}